  "preamble": "Assibilation of the voiced palatal stop [ɟ] to the alveolar affricate [dz]. The clearest case is West Slavic: the Proto-Slavic reflex of *dj, written *ď and usually taken as a palatal stop [ɟ(ː)], became dz in Polish and Slovak (*meďa > miedza, medza 'balk'), against Slovene j, Serbo-Croatian đ and East Slavic ž. In Czech and Sorbian the same dz went on to z (mez, Upper Sorbian přaza). The phonetic value of *ď is debated (a palatal stop or a palatalized dental [dʲ]), so the same data are also filed under [dʲ] > [dz]. Only one subgroup shows the change, so it is rare.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Assibilation, Depalatalization",
